WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.263
VideoComposer, from Alibaba and Ant Group,

00:00:03.343 --> 00:00:07.055
makes video generation controllable through composition.

00:00:07.135 --> 00:00:11.387
Users combine textual, spatial, and temporal
conditions at once.

00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:17.006
Its key ideas: motion vectors from compressed video
as an explicit temporal signal,

00:00:17.086 --> 00:00:24.318
and a unified Spatio-Temporal Condition encoder
that fuses diverse controls while keeping frames consistent,

00:00:24.398 --> 00:00:28.380
from text and sketches to reference video or simple
strokes.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:34.401
Diffusion models made image generation highly
controllable, but video is much harder.

00:00:34.481 --> 00:00:39.314
Video adds a temporal axis: motion patterns vary
enormously across clips,

00:00:39.394 --> 00:00:43.150
and every frame must stay consistent with its neighbours.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:49.544
Reusing the spatial controls designed for images
gives no reliable handle on temporal dynamics,

00:00:49.624 --> 00:00:53.380
so controllable video synthesis stayed an open challenge.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.728
To control a video, you must control its motion,
not only its appearance.

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:01.286
The authors' insight: motion vectors,

00:01:01.366 --> 00:01:06.472
computed inside compressed video to encode
inter-frame change, are a cheap,

00:01:06.552 --> 00:01:09.376
explicit description of temporal dynamics.

00:01:09.456 --> 00:01:14.355
Turning them into a control signal lets users
prescribe how things move.

00:01:14.435 --> 00:01:19.541
But mixing them with sketches, depth, and images
naively hurts consistency,

00:01:19.621 --> 00:01:22.860
motivating a unified encoder for space and time.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.446
VideoComposer makes three contributions.

00:01:26.526 --> 00:01:29.732
First,

00:01:29.812 --> 00:01:33.018
it frames video generation as compositional: a user
supplies any subset of textual,

00:01:33.098 --> 00:01:36.304
spatial, and temporal conditions,

00:01:36.384 --> 00:01:39.590
and the model recomposes a video obeying all of them.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.876
Second,

00:01:42.956 --> 00:01:46.162
it introduces motion vectors from compressed video
as an explicit temporal control signal.

00:01:46.242 --> 00:01:49.448
Third, it proposes the Spatio-Temporal Condition encoder,

00:01:49.528 --> 00:01:52.734
a lightweight module that turns every sequential
condition into a shared representation

00:01:52.814 --> 00:01:56.020
while boosting temporal consistency.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.388
VideoComposer is a latent diffusion model that
denoises video in a compressed latent space.

00:02:02.468 --> 00:02:07.522
Each training video is decomposed into three
families of conditions, textual,

00:02:07.602 --> 00:02:10.122
spatial, and temporal, combined freely.

00:02:10.202 --> 00:02:14.189
Sequential conditions like motion vectors, depth
maps, masks,

00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:21.857
and sketches all pass through one shared
STC-encoder: two convolutions and pooling capture local spatial structure,

00:02:21.937 --> 00:02:25.591
then a temporal Transformer models change across frames.

00:02:25.671 --> 00:02:30.192
Encoded conditions are summed and concatenated with
the noisy latent,

00:02:30.272 --> 00:02:33.592
while text and style enter through cross-attention.

00:02:33.672 --> 00:02:35.459
Training runs in two stages,

00:02:35.539 --> 00:02:39.660
and inference uses DDIM sampling with
classifier-free guidance.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.528
VideoComposer is trained on two public datasets:
WebVid-10M,

00:02:44.608 --> 00:02:49.097
about ten million video-caption pairs from the web,
and LAION-400M,

00:02:49.177 --> 00:02:53.665
CLIP-filtered image-caption pairs for visual quality.

00:02:53.745 --> 00:02:58.233
For evaluation,

00:02:58.313 --> 00:03:02.802
the authors report text-to-video on MSR-VTT using
Fréchet Video Distance and CLIP similarity,

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:07.370
and measure motion controllability on a thousand
caption-video pairs with a dedicated motion-control error.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.952
On MSR-VTT,

00:03:11.032 --> 00:03:14.234
VideoComposer achieves a Fréchet Video Distance of
five hundred eighty and a CLIP similarity

00:03:14.314 --> 00:03:17.517
of zero point two nine three two,

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:20.799
zero-shot.

00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:24.081
That is competitive with leading text-to-video systems,

00:03:24.161 --> 00:03:27.363
and it improves over the model's first-stage pre-training,

00:03:27.443 --> 00:03:30.646
which scored eight hundred three.

00:03:30.726 --> 00:03:33.928
So adding compositional, multi-condition control
costs nothing in quality,

00:03:34.008 --> 00:03:37.210
while videos follow the sketches, depth, motion,
and styles users provide.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:40.700
The ablations isolate where control originates.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:45.452
Using only text gives a motion-control error of
four point zero three.

00:03:45.532 --> 00:03:50.747
Adding motion vectors as a temporal condition drops
it to two point six seven,

00:03:50.827 --> 00:03:56.449
and enabling the Spatio-Temporal Condition encoder
lowers it to two point one eight.

00:03:56.529 --> 00:03:59.571
Both matter: motion vectors supply the signal,

00:03:59.651 --> 00:04:02.490
and the STC-encoder makes the model use it.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:06.520
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.250
On MSR-VTT,

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:13.980
VideoComposer scores a Fréchet Video Distance of
five hundred eighty and a CLIP similarity

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.710
of zero point two nine three two.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:21.440
Its motion-control error falls to two point one eight,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.170
versus four point zero three for a text-only baseline.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:28.900
And all is learned from two datasets, WebVid-10M
and LAION-400M.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.382
The takeaway: video generation becomes more
controllable when you treat a video as a composition

00:04:35.462 --> 00:04:38.538
of conditions and give motion an explicit signal.

00:04:38.618 --> 00:04:44.720
VideoComposer combines compressed-video motion
vectors with a Spatio-Temporal Condition encoder,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.130
so users steer content and motion together, from
text, sketches, and depth to masks,

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:54.380
reference videos, or two strokes, while keeping
frames consistent.
